one pregnant who develops a rash or has contact with a rash illness should promptly contact their maternity or medical team.","quality_score":"4","adoption":"RUBELLA_07","direct_score_input":false},{"row":78,"status":"PASS_RUBELLA_08","source":"UKHSA rubella and pregnancy protection","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/viral-rash-in-pregnancy/rash-in-pregnancy-quick-reference-for-health-professionals","verified":"Suspected measles or rubella during pregnancy should be notified urgently to the health protection team.","quality_score":"4","adoption":"RUBELLA_08","direct_score_input":false},{"row":79,"status":"PASS_WITH_RUBELLA_09","source":"UKHSA rubella and pregnancy protection","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/rubella-the-green-book-chapter","verified":"Rubella risk depends on immunity, pregnancy stage, exposure, contact timing, diagnostic evidence and vaccination history.","quality_score":"3","adoption":"RUBELLA_GUARD_09","direct_score_input":false},{"row":80,"status":"PASS_WITH_RUBELLA_10","source":"UKHSA rubella and pregnancy protection","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/rubella-the-green-book-chapter","verified":"Rubella evidence is case-, pregnancy-, contact-, vaccination- and time-specific and is not an exact parcel-level congenital-harm probability.","quality_score":"3","adoption":"RUBELLA_GUARD_10","direct_score_input":false}]}